Z||000003||b lo 0||07-21-2000||11:40 AM||||Good job!!  I'm a pro installer and distributor, and I somehow managed to cram a JL Audio 12W6 behind the seat in my '85.  Oh, I have one discrepancy with your install-using plywood is a BIG NO-NO!!  Especially on a wall with that much surface area.  I hope you've got it braced like crazy on the inside, or that thing's gonna flex more than a $5,000 prostitute and give you all kinds of distortion.  I would have built that with MDF (medium density fiberboard) like I always do.  It's a little more expensive, but it's way stronger and less flexible.  I know a guy who put 3 10W1-4s in a plywood enclosure and the box literally exploded from the pressure of the subs.  Not a pretty sight-or feeling-I had to help him clean the shards of wood out of the back of his CRX.  I don't think that'll be a problem with only one 10", but I definitely think you'll get all kinds of distortion.  I'd pull out the sub and put braces on the inside since you've probably already carpeted the thing.  Good job with the sound dampener, though!!  Try my company site at <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/ga3/audioimagery" TARGET=_blank>www.angelfire.com/ga3/audioimagery</A>  for great stereo stuff.<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT (in progress)  <BR>titled ICE-plates B LO 0 <BR>GAWD I love this car||||reg||1
Z||000004||coyote||07-21-2000||12:09 PM||||the beige lines are a factory sealent that they use over material joints, actually this was a fairly simple job, and the rag top has aquired a donor top from a cavaler that i will be fitting this winter. as far as flex i am not to worried, it is 3/4 cdx with fiberglassed stiffeners running top to bottom, actually fired it up last night with very ittle distortion even at 300 plus watts and no dicernable flexing.<P>coyote||||reg||1
Z||000005||b lo 0||07-21-2000||12:51 PM||||Good job with the stiffeners, Coyote.  The thing is though, I've got a 12w6 (probably the same specs as your 10, requires about the same air) in a 3 cubic foot enclosure of MDF, absolutely no bracing, same style construction, with almost NO flex-tested with laser and mirror-try it-and NO distortion running 450 watts through the one 12.  If you wanna try the laser flex test, take a small pocket-sized section of mirror (mines acrylic), if the enclosure is already carpeted put the hook part of Velcro in the back, it it's not use wood glue-it comes off of the acrylic, then stick the mirror to the largest wall of the enclosure, then mount a commercial laser (some places rent them, check the Yellow Pages under scientific supply) to a camera tripod so it shines onto the mirror.  Put an easel so the reflected beam shines in the middle, about 5 feet from the enclosure, fire the sub up at max volume with a resonance test CD, then measure the largest the beam spreads on the chart on the easel.  Sounds complicated, but it's really simple, and I do it to all the enclosures I build on a test bench.  You can get a test CD for about 8-10 bucks at best buy, look in the hip-hop or compilation sections, and look for Bass Mekanik.  Lots of test tracks, as well as some good bass-heavy techno-style tracks.  Happy thumping!<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT (in progress)  <BR>titled ICE-plates B LO 0 <BR>GAWD I love this car||||reg||1
